{"records":[{"id":"h6mrnn4zvb","title":"Correction to Mengwasser KE, Adeyemi RO, Leng Y et al. Molecular Cell, 2019","description":"In our paper, we reported a striking dependence of BRCA2-deficient cells on the function of the apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) endonuclease Ape2 (APEX2). Our paper included rescue experiments with various mutants to verify this finding and to clarify the mechanism. In Figures 4I-J, for the gRNA-resistant APEX2 mutant that lacks a functional PIP-box PCNA-binding motif, we inadvertently tested APEX2 R339A instead of APEX2 Y396A/F397A. \n\nConsequently, we generated the gRNA-resistant Y396A/F397A APEX2 mutant and tested its ability to rescue the growth defect caused by gRNA-mediated APEX2 depletion, in ovarian BRCA2 mutant cells. In contrast to the result shown in Figures 4I-J, we found that the PIP-box double mutant (Y396A/F397A) does rescue the growth defect caused by gRNA-mediated depletion of APEX2. Thus, we conclude that while the catalytic activity of APEX2 is required for its ability to promote the survival of BRCA2 mutant cells, its PCNA-binding activity is not. We apologize for this error, however it does not affect the overall observation of our paper regarding BRCA2's synthetic lethality with APEX2 loss.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-30T02:24:08.143Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h6mrnn4zvb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h6mrnn4zvb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kristen","last_name":"Mengwasser","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Richard","last_name":"Adeyemi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumei","last_name":"Leng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mei Yuk","last_name":"Choi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephen","last_name":"Elledge","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Correction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t6g8bfpgvk","title":"M. Jost et al,. Mol Cell 2017 - Figure 5D - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figure 5D of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-08-29T10:28:07.227Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t6g8bfpgvk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t6g8bfpgvk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ankit","last_name":"Rai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Akhmanova","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","In Vitro Techniques"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"dfdxy4ksh3","title":"Figure 4 - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figures 4 and S4 of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-07-18T06:50:43.254Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dfdxy4ksh3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dfdxy4ksh3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marvin","last_name":"Tanenbaum","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Microscopy"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"tm22dtht9x","title":"M. Jost et al,. Mol Cell 2017 - Figure 5A - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figure 5A of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. Mol. Cell. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-08-29T10:27:52.938Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tm22dtht9x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tm22dtht9x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","In Vitro Techniques"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fpwtnn9yp8","title":"CancerSSDTs","description":"CancerSSDTs\nThis repository contains data, codes, and supplementary information used to identify strictly-selective drug targets for 13 cancers.\n\nRequirements\nThe following tools are needed:\n\nCOBRA Toolbox\nrFASTCORMICS\nCPLEX v12.9.0 or any CPLEX version compatible with MATLAB.\n\nTo Obtain Strictly Selective Lethal Sets, Follow the Steps Below:\nInitialize COBRA toolbox:\ninitCobraToolbox\nUse rFASTCORMICS to build normal and cancer context-specific models for each tissue. The parameters for this step are provided in BuildCSM.m. Provided models are available in Contex_Specific_Models:\nBuildCSM.m\nUse RapidSL to identify single and synthetic lethal drug targets for all cancers. A sample script is provided in RunRapidSL.m. All identified selective and non-selective solutions are available in All_Identified_Synthetic_Lethal_Non_Selective:\nRunRapidSL.m\nUse the FindSSDTs.m function to extract strictly-selective drug targets for each cancer. A sample use of this function is provided in IdentifySelectives.m:\nIdentifySelectives.m\nAll SSDTs identified in this study are available in the Supplementary_File_S1:\nThis repository represents the data related to the following submitted manuscript:\nManshadi et al.,\"Higher order synthetic lethals are keys to minimizing cancer treatment effects on normal cells\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-13T08:41:33.35Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fpwtnn9yp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fpwtnn9yp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"mehdi","last_name":"Dehghan Manshadi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Payam","last_name":"Setoodeh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amin Reza","last_name":"Rajabzadeh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amin","last_name":"Ramezani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Habil","last_name":"Zare","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Software"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cx297hzkjk","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled: \"Endogenous DNA 3' blocks are vulnerabilities for BRCA1 and BRCA2 deficiency and are reversed by the APE2 nuclease\". Alvarez-Quilon et al. 2020","description":"Primary data for the paper entitled \"Endogenous DNA 3' blocks are vulnerabilities for BRCA1 and BRCA2 deficiency and are reversed by the APE2 nuclease\" published in Molecular Cell (DOI: 10.1016/j.molcel.2020.05.021).\n\nThe dataset includes:\n1) Uncropped blot images.\n2) the sgRNA readcounts for the CRISPR screens included in this work.\n3) Primary values used for all graphs included in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T16:48:57.828Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cx297hzkjk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cx297hzkjk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Alvarez-Quilon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"42sxkx9sdb","title":"Synthetic lethality-based prediction of anti-SARS-CoV-2 targets. Pal et al","description":"Supplementary data for the paper: \"Synthetic lethality-based prediction of anti-SARS-CoV-2 targets\". \nTable S1: Enriched Reactome pathways for DE gene sets (FDR<0.1). (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 2).\nTable S2: List of synthetic lethal pairs in each of the transcriptomic dataset used in this study and the list of unified 454 SL/SDL partner genes. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 1).\nTable S3: List of 140 candidate targets and three subsets comprising known drug targets, hub SL/SDL partner genes when each one of them are paired up with multiple DE genes and genes with enriched Reactome pathways.  Pathway enrichment analysis (FDR <0.1) for SL/SDL partner genes, DE genes and combination pathway enrichment analysis for DE gene-SL/SDL partner gene pairs for 140 candidates. List of 135 candidates overlapping with genome-wide siRNA screen. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 1 and 2).\nTable S4: Targeted siRNA screen results for selected 26 targets and annotation of 26 candidate targets with reference to SARS-CoV-2 or any other virus in the literature. (Related to STAR Methods and Figure 3).\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-19T18:44:35.774Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/42sxkx9sdb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/42sxkx9sdb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lipika","last_name":"R. Pal","orcid":"0000-0002-3390-110X"},{"first_name":"Kuoyuan","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nishanth Ulhas","last_name":"Nair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Martin-Sancho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanju","last_name":"Sinha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan","last_name":"Pu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Riva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fiorella","last_name":"Schischlik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joo Sang","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sumit K.","last_name":"Chanda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eytan","last_name":"Ruppin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptome","Drug","Antiviral Therapy","CRISPR/Cas9","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121525,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":129,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121525,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120966,"restricted":63,"embargoed":496},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2122},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":630},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":438},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":403},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":389},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":301},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":247},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":212}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119122},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1713},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":174},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":125},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida",